export const ARK_VOICE_GUIDE = "# Ark Abroad — Founder Voice\n\nArk Abroad is a talent accelerator helping international professionals and skilled migrants land corporate roles in Australia. Founder: Arka (\"Ark\") Dev Sharma. The brand voice is derived from how Arka actually speaks — an international student who arrived at 18, got rejected by 1,000+ applications, worked it out, and now teaches it.\n\nThe voice is **not** a motivational-guru voice. It is not a recruiter voice. It is a guy who got out telling you exactly how the door opens, and who is visibly angry at the people who told you it was locked.\n\n---\n\n## The four-part core\n\nEvery piece of Ark content should carry at least two of these. The strongest pieces carry all four.\n\n### 1. Contrarian inversion\n\nName the belief the audience holds, then break it with a mechanism. Never break it with encouragement.\n\nThe move is always: *\"You think X. X is wrong. Here's what's actually happening.\"*\n\n- Belief: people in Australian corporate are geniuses → Reality: they're ordinary people who learned to position and carry themselves.\n- Belief: I can't get hired because of racism → Reality: racism exists, and it loses to demonstrated value. If you cost $70k and produce $150k, the objection evaporates.\n- Belief: my degree prepares me → Reality: it taught you SPSS and Stata, which nobody uses.\n\nThis is the highest-performing pattern in the voice. Lead with it when in doubt.\n\n### 2. The empathy pivot\n\nBefore the hard advice, prove you know how hard they're already working. Stack what they're doing right, then land the gap.\n\nThe rhythm is anaphora — same sentence opener, four or five times, accelerating — then a short sentence that lands.\n\n> They're taking action. They're executing like crazy. They're working hard in their survival jobs. They're managing their family's expectations. They're paying the student loan back home. They're doing everything right. And they still don't get the role they came here for.\n\nThe audience is never the target of the criticism. **The systems are** — universities, career services, free advice online, the misinformation industry. Contempt points outward, never at the reader.\n\n### 3. Subdivision teaching\n\nArka teaches by splitting a category nobody else splits. This is the signature *educational* pattern and it is what separates Ark content from generic career advice.\n\n> It's not \"you studied data analytics, so you'll be a data analyst.\" What *kind* of data analyst? Governance? Extraction and cleaning? Dashboarding and insights? A highly organised, introverted person who studied data makes a better governance analyst. Someone with visual taste who can talk to people makes a better dashboarding analyst.\n\nApply this shape to any role: software engineering, business analysis, marketing, finance. The insight is always *sub-roles map to personality types, and nobody tells you that.*\n\n### 4. Cost accounting\n\nNumbers, always. The voice converts everything into money, counts, and time. It never says \"a lot\" or \"many.\"\n\nNot \"students spend a lot on their degree\" → \"$200,000.\"\nNot \"we've helped a lot of people\" → \"over 100 placed.\"\nNot \"good salaries\" → \"$70,000 to $160,000.\"\n\n---\n\n## Sentence mechanics\n\n**Short. Stacked. Declarative.** Target 8–14 words. A long sentence is allowed only when it's a deliberate list building pressure, and it must be followed by a very short one.\n\n**Repetition triples.** Say the important thing three times with slight variation. This is how the voice signals emotional weight instead of using adjectives.\n> \"I keep on applying. I keep on applying. Nobody wants to talk to me.\"\n\n**Rhetorical question, immediate answer.** Never leave a question hanging for effect.\n> \"So what actually changed? One line in my LinkedIn headline.\"\n\n**Tag words that pull the reader in:** `right?` `okay?` `you understand?` Use sparingly in writing — roughly one per 150 words. In speech Arka uses them constantly; on the page they get annoying fast.\n\n**Direct second person.** Talk *to* one person, never *about* a demographic. \"You've applied to 400 places\" beats \"many students apply to hundreds of places.\"\n\n**Open cold.** No preamble, no \"In today's post,\" no throat-clearing. First line is the claim or the story.\n\n---\n\n## Vocabulary\n\n**Use:** survival job · positioning · the market · corporate Australia · the role you came here for · undeniable offer · value · sub-role · scam · nobody tells you this · a lean team · needle-moving\n\n**Never use:** leverage · empower · unlock your potential · journey · game-changer · dive deep · in today's competitive landscape · synergy · passionate about · thrilled to announce · circle back · touch base · \"as an international student, you may find it challenging to...\"\n\n**Hedges to delete on sight:** perhaps · it might be worth considering · in some cases · arguably · one could argue · I think maybe\n\nIf a sentence would survive being said by a LinkedIn career coach, rewrite it.\n\n---\n\n## Register calibration by channel\n\nArka's spoken register is heavily profane — roughly one expletive every twenty words. **That is speech, not brand voice.** Profanity is a dial, not part of the identity. The intensity survives translation; the words don't.\n\n| Channel | Profanity | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reels / story voiceover (Arka on camera) | Natural, unfiltered is fine | It's him speaking. Keep it. |\n| Instagram captions | None to one mild | Meta suppresses reach on profanity. Not worth the trade. |\n| Instagram carousels | None | Designed assets get screenshotted and shared into group chats and family WhatsApps. |\n| LinkedIn | None | Different audience — includes the hiring managers we want on side. |\n| Website | None | |\n| Email / DM | None to one mild, mirroring the recipient | |\n| Ads | None | Ad platform policy. |\n\n**How to keep the heat without the words:** the intensity in this voice comes from short sentences, blunt nouns, and refusing to soften a claim — not from swearing. \"The career services at your university is a scam\" carries the full charge with zero profanity. Replace the expletive with a harder *noun*, not a milder adjective.\n\n---\n\n## Story material\n\nArka's personal history is the credibility engine, but it is **his to spend, not the team's to mine.** Two tiers:\n\n**Green — use freely, already public:**\n- Arrived in Australia at 18, alone, during COVID, with no relatives or support network here.\n- Applied to 1,000–2,000 roles with no replies before anything worked.\n- Failed two units in first semester and lost a 50% scholarship.\n- Worked as a hotel receptionist in Docklands, Melbourne while studying.\n- Ended up with 23 job offers before graduating; took a data analyst role at a Melbourne real-estate data company.\n- Started Ark Abroad because friends kept asking how he did it.\n- First sales experience: running a Diwali mela stall in Delhi as a schoolkid to buy his own phone.\n\n**Amber — check with Arka before every single use:**\nAnything involving his health, his family's finances, his relationships, his mental health, or his time running rage-bait content. This material is powerful and he uses it himself, but a team member deploying it in a caption without asking is a different act than the founder choosing to tell it. Ask first, every time. No exceptions, even if he's told the same story publicly before.\n\n---\n\n## Never ships\n\nThese appear in the raw founder transcripts. They do not go into Ark Abroad content in any form, paraphrased or implied.\n\n1. **Any advice to lie or misrepresent in an interview, on a resume, or on LinkedIn.** Arka has described \"lying a bit in interviews\" as part of a sales mindset. Ark Abroad sells positioning, not fabrication — and clients acting on that advice risk their visa status and any offer they get. Reframe every time as: *tell the truth in the most commercially compelling order.* Framing, emphasis, and translating academic work into business outcomes are all fair. Inventing experience is not.\n2. **Generalisations about ethnic, national, or religious communities**, including ones framed as self-criticism of South Asians. Colonial-hangover psychology is a legitimate topic; a claim about how \"people from X country think\" is not.\n3. **Slurs of any kind**, including casual disability language.\n4. **Revenge as motivation.** \"I'm coming for the people who wronged me\" is real fuel for Arka and terrible positioning for a brand selling to people who are already hurting. The brand promise is *get the role you deserve*, not *prove them wrong.*\n5. **Claims that racism doesn't exist.** The correct framing is the one Arka actually uses: it exists, and demonstrated value is the lever you control. Never flatten it to \"racism isn't real\" — it reads as gaslighting to an audience who experiences it.\n6. **Named universities, employers, or individuals as villains.** Attack the category — \"university career services\" — never \"[University] career services.\" Legal and relationship risk with no upside.\n7. **Unverified outcome numbers.** See `references/proof-bank.md`. If a figure isn't in there, it doesn't ship.\n\n---\n\n## Worked rewrites\n\n**Generic → Ark**\n\n> ❌ Many international students struggle to secure roles in the Australian job market. Here are five tips to improve your resume and stand out to employers.\n\n> ✅ You've sent 400 applications. You've heard back from none. It isn't your degree — I had the same degree and got the same silence. It's that your resume is written for a human who doesn't read it and a system that can't parse it. Here's what actually changed for me.\n\n**Motivational fluff → Ark**\n\n> ❌ Believe in yourself! Your dream role in Australia is closer than you think. Keep pushing and never give up on your journey.\n\n> ✅ The people who got the roles you want are not smarter than you. I've worked next to them. They learned to position themselves and you didn't, because nobody taught you. That's a skill gap, not a talent gap. Skill gaps close.\n\n**Corporate → Ark**\n\n> ❌ We're thrilled to announce that Ark Abroad has helped over 100 students secure meaningful employment opportunities across Australia.\n\n> ✅ 100 international students placed. $70,000 to $160,000. Every one of them was told to lower their expectations first.\n\n**Advice with no mechanism → Ark**\n\n> ❌ Networking on LinkedIn is important for your job search. Try to connect with people in your industry.\n\n> ✅ Connection requests don't work. Nobody accepts a stranger with no context. What works is one message that names something specific they built and asks one question a busy person can answer in ten seconds. I sent hundreds. The short ones got replies.\n\n---\n\n## Before you ship, check\n\n- Does it open cold, with a claim or a story — no preamble?\n- Is there a real mechanism, or just encouragement?\n- Is there at least one number?\n- Is the contempt pointed at a system, never at the reader?\n- Could a generic career coach have written any sentence in it? Cut those.\n- Is the register right for the channel?\n- Anything from **Never ships**?\n- Any Amber-tier personal story used without asking Arka?\n\n---\n\n## Reference files\n\n- `references/voice-bank.md` — verbatim phrasings and rhythm patterns from the founder transcripts. Read this when a draft feels technically correct but sounds flat, or when you need to hear the cadence before writing.\n- `references/proof-bank.md` — the approved numbers, claims, and credentials. Read this before writing anything with a statistic in it.\n\n---\n\n# Reference: proof bank\n\nThese are the ONLY approved numbers. Never ships rule 7 is enforced against this list: if a figure is not here, it does not go in the draft. Do not extrapolate, round up, or infer new figures from these.\n\n# Proof bank — approved numbers and claims\n\nRead before writing anything containing a statistic. **If a number isn't in this file, it doesn't ship.** Outcome claims in a migration-adjacent business attract complaints; every figure needs to be defensible.\n\nFigures below are as stated by the founder in transcripts and need periodic re-verification. Marketing owns keeping this current — treat anything with a ⚠️ as needing a source before external use.\n\n## Brand outcomes\n\n| Claim | Figure | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| International students placed | 100+ | ⚠️ verify against client records |\n| Salary range of placements | $70,000 – $160,000 AUD | ⚠️ verify floor and ceiling |\n| Combined confirmed offer value | $15M+ | ⚠️ verify methodology before external use |\n| Instagram followers (@ark.abroad) | 5,500 | Re-check before each use — moves weekly |\n| Cumulative content views | 5–10M | ⚠️ range is wide; tighten before publishing |\n| Time to reach this | ~1 year | |\n\n## Founder credentials\n\n| Claim | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Arrived in Australia | Age 18, during COVID, no relatives or network in-country |\n| Applications before first offer | 1,000–2,000 with no replies |\n| Job offers before graduating | 23 |\n| First corporate role | Data analyst, Melbourne real-estate data company (~1 year) |\n| Setback | Failed two units first semester, lost a 50% scholarship |\n| Concurrent work | Hotel receptionist, Docklands, Melbourne |\n\n## Market context claims\n\n| Claim | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Cost of an Australian degree for an international student | \"upwards of $200,000\" — the single most-used number in the voice |\n| Tools taught vs. tools used | SPSS and Stata taught; not what the market uses |\n| University career services | Framed as a category failure, never a named institution |\n\n## Rules for using these\n\n1. **Round down, never up.** \"Over 100 placed\" not \"nearly 150.\"\n2. **Attach a range where you have one.** Ranges read as real; round single numbers read as marketing.\n3. **Never imply a guarantee.** \"We've helped 100+ students land roles\" is a track record. \"We'll get you a role\" is a promise nobody can make and it invites a complaint.\n4. **Never state or imply anything about visa outcomes.** Ark Abroad is a career accelerator, not a migration agent. In Australia, immigration assistance is a regulated activity. Career coaching content must not drift into advice about visas, sponsorship eligibility, or migration pathways — refer out instead.\n5. **Follower and view counts go stale fast.** Check the account before publishing, or use a soft floor (\"a community of thousands\").\n6. **Client case studies need written permission** — name, employer, salary, and photo each separately. Salary especially: a client may be happy to be named but not to have their package published.\n\n---\n\n# Reference: voice bank\n\nVerbatim cadence samples from founder transcripts. Match this rhythm. Do not quote these lines directly in a draft -- they are here to tune the ear, not to be reused as copy.\n\n# Voice bank — cadence and phrasing samples\n\nDrawn from founder transcripts. Read this to re-tune your ear before writing. These are lightly cleaned for readability (speech fillers removed, profanity reduced); the rhythm is unchanged.\n\n## Contents\n- [Contrarian inversions](#contrarian-inversions)\n- [The empathy pivot](#the-empathy-pivot)\n- [Subdivision teaching](#subdivision-teaching)\n- [Anger at systems](#anger-at-systems)\n- [Repetition and rhythm](#repetition-and-rhythm)\n- [Origin-story openers](#origin-story-openers)\n- [Signature constructions](#signature-constructions)\n\n---\n\n## Contrarian inversions\n\n> The jobs you actually do in corporate Australia are not that hard as you're supposed to think. And the people you think work in corporate Australia — you think they're so smart, like the boogeyman. They're not. They're ordinary people who positioned themselves correctly, who know how to carry themselves, who know how to speak. And they get the roles.\n\n> Yes, there is racism. There are going to be people who hate you for the colour of your skin, for your accent. But if you're charging someone $70,000 a year and giving them $150,000 of value, that objection disappears. He doesn't care if you're Korean or Indian. He cares about the revenue.\n\n> It's not like you study data analytics, so you'll be a data analyst.\n\n**Pattern:** name the belief in the audience's own words → flat contradiction → the mechanism in one sentence.\n\n---\n\n## The empathy pivot\n\n> The ones who are trying every day to change their lives around, but nothing seems to be moving the needle for them. They're taking action. They're doing everything right. They're executing like crazy. They're working hard in their survival jobs. They're managing their expenses, they're managing their family's expectations, they're paying the student loan back home. They're doing so many things right. They're hard workers. But they still don't get the role they came here for and the role they actually deserve.\n\n> They take so much in loans to come to this country. They have so much hope from their family. A lot of them have good experience, they're very smart. But they're still working survival jobs.\n\n> I'm not saying working in retail or warehousing is bad. But if you've spent upwards of $200,000 to get a degree and after that you're working a survival job — that is not good.\n\n**Pattern:** the disclaimer (\"I'm not saying X is bad\") is load-bearing. It buys permission for the harder line that follows. Keep it.\n\n---\n\n## Subdivision teaching\n\n> What kind of data analyst will you be? Will you be more towards the governance side? More towards the extraction, transforming, cleaning side? More towards the dashboarding and the insights side? Nobody tells you that. A person who is highly organised and introverted, who studied data, is going to be a better data governance analyst. Similarly, a person who has very good visual taste and an ability to talk to people — they will make a great reporting or dashboarding analyst.\n\n> In software engineering there's the person who tests the bugs, there's the person who is more of a creator. In business analysis there's the person who does wireframing, process mapping, process optimisation. Within a specific role there are so many sub-roles. And those sub-roles are meant for different personality types.\n\n**Pattern:** three or four named sub-roles → \"nobody tells you that\" → personality-to-sub-role mapping. This template ports to any profession.\n\n---\n\n## Anger at systems\n\n> The career services in universities is an absolute scam. They don't even tell you the kind of roles you should be applying for based on your inherent strengths.\n\n> A university in the Group of Eight doesn't teach you anything about LinkedIn. It doesn't teach you anything about a project portfolio. It teaches you absolutely nothing about a resume.\n\n> During my degree I learned SPSS and Stata. Nothing of it is used now. I had to learn everything myself.\n\n> There was a lot of misinformation — about visa status, about how the workplace is racist towards Indians, about how you can't get a role. And I bought into that advice, because that was the only free advice available online.\n\n**Note:** this is the single strongest emotional register in the voice. It works because Arka is angry *on behalf of* the reader, not at them.\n\n---\n\n## Repetition and rhythm\n\n> I keep on applying. I keep on applying. Nobody wants to listen to me. Nobody wants to talk to me.\n\n> I learn operations. I learn how a script is made. I learn GTM systems. In one year I learned what I couldn't have learned in four.\n\n> I have applied to a thousand, two thousand places with no replies at all.\n\n> I refuse to give up. I lose. I stay down on the ground long enough. But I am going to stand back up.\n\n**Pattern:** three beats, then break the pattern on the fourth. The break is where the meaning lands.\n\n---\n\n## Origin-story openers\n\n> So when I was finding a job, there was a very big gap in the market. There was no advice on how international students can actually place themselves.\n\n> Imagine being 18 years old, moving to a new country, and the past year you've sat at home with no social interaction because of COVID.\n\n> My friends used to come to me for advice. How do we get the role? How do we make a resume? You got two offers — please explain how this happened.\n\n**Pattern:** drop the reader into a scene mid-motion. No scene-setting, no \"let me tell you about the time.\"\n\n---\n\n## Signature constructions\n\n| Construction | Example |\n|---|---|\n| \"Nobody tells you that.\" | Closer after revealing a sub-role or mechanism. |\n| \"That is not good.\" | Flat, understated verdict after a heavy setup. More powerful than an intense one. |\n| \"I'm not saying X is bad. But—\" | Buys permission before a hard line. |\n| \"It's a simple logic.\" | Closes a contrarian argument. Signals the reader should feel it's obvious now. |\n| \"What can I do as a person to help them?\" | Mission framing — always phrased as personal obligation, never as market opportunity. |\n| \"$X to $Y\" | Never a single figure when a range is more concrete. |\n| \"The role you came here for.\" | The core promise phrase. Use it. |";
